Reasoning:
Okay, so I've just made some key additions to the `task-web/pom.xml`. My thinking was pretty straightforward: I need the core web functionality, so `spring-boot-starter-web` was the 
obvious choice. That pulls in all the essentials for building web applications with Spring Boot, including the necessary servlet APIs and Spring MVC components.

Beyond just the web layer, I also realized I'll need to handle validation. For that, I added `spring-boot-starter-validation`. This is crucial for ensuring data integrity at the API 
boundaries, and it brings in the `jakarta.validation` API, which is the standard for this kind of thing.

Lastly, I included `spring-boot-starter-actuator`. This is more about the operational side of things. I want to be able to monitor and manage the application once it's running, and 
Actuator provides all the endpoints for health checks, metrics, and other useful operational data.

The important part here, and why I didn't explicitly define versions for any of these starters, is my reliance on the `spring-boot-starter-parent`. That parent POM is meticulously 
configured to manage the versions of all these dependencies, ensuring compatibility and avoiding version conflicts. It's a cornerstone of Spring Boot's opinionated approach, and by 
leveraging it, I've significantly simplified my POM management.

With those dependencies in place, my next logical step is to attempt a build. I'm confident these additions will provide the foundational pieces I need for the `task-web` module, and now 
I just need to verify that everything integrates correctly.

